WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:12.800
I accidentally found the most mind blowing
endgame theory on the Japanese Internet.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.660
Also you guys forced me to sing Bink’s sake
on Camera to celebrate one HUNDRED thousand

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.660
subscribers.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.730
All of this and more in this video.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.720
What’s up everybody.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.500
Ohara here and today is a good day.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.449
Because I have, not just one, but TWO real
goodies for you today.

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:43.660
And I personally definitely know which one
I’m more excited about.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.260
Nope not that part.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.190
You’ll get to witness me questioning my
life choices in real time if you make ‘till

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.370
the end of this video.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.310
The bad news for me is: You will, because
it’s a damn good video.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:01.320
I’ll summarise how we got here as quickly
as possible, so we can get to the juicy bits.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.930
Let’s put 30 seconds on the clock:
We reached 100K subs.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.430
I asked you guys to come up with a video idea
to celebrate

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.970
You guys picked singing Binks Sake over Eating
Bread.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.540
Then I thought to myself: Hm.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.430
Instead of torturing you guys right from the
start why not talk a bit about the song itself

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.430
a bit.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.860
I think there might be a thing or two in there
that might be interesting.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.640
So I looked it up.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.140
And what I stumbled upon blew my fucking mind.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.610
Was that 30 seconds?

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.220
Damn.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.140
Okay let’s cut to the chase.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.510
The premise of this theory is from a Japanese
One Piece fan who goes by the name Chipiko,

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:52.140
mixed with my own ideas and other random stuff
I found on the interweb.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.510
That guy.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.650
Went through the original Japanese lyrics
of the song and gave his interpretation for

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.070
each important line.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.979
Without giving too much of the mind-blow that
I definitely had away, the basic idea is that

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:07.570
this song talks about the final island of
the grand line.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.560
Laugh Tale.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.170
Wait!

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.430
Before you click away already, let me also
spoil the last line of the song.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:23.260
In the English translation it goes ‘Never-Ending,
Ever-wandering, Our funny Traveling tale!’

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.579
That already sounds a bit like Laugh tale.

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:30.109
But in the Japanese ORIGINAL it’s actually
even more in your face.

00:02:30.109 --> 00:02:35.279
The word Oda used, Waraibanashi, literally
means a laugh tale.

00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:36.279
Literally.

00:02:36.279 --> 00:02:40.591
Like, that’s so straight-forward, that it
actually makes me wonder whether Oda didn’t

00:02:40.591 --> 00:02:47.749
just put this into 2003 Google translate and
got Laugh Tale as a result and just went with

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:54.269
it.

00:02:54.269 --> 00:03:00.069
After all Oda put a ridiculous amount of thought
into this and had the song ready many years

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:04.069
before we actually reached Thriller Bark,
while the island itself is first mentioned

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:07.370
as far back as chapter 105.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.180
I hope that convinces you that this song is
not only a good chance to humiliate a poor

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.370
YouTuber, but also carries a deeper meaning
hinting at the climax of the story.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.959
Let’s start with the very first line of
the song.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:22.489
Let’s go and deliver Bink’s sake.

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:24.680
This already brings up 2 important questions.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.159
Who the bleep is Binks?

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:29.739
And what the bleep is his sake?

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:35.049
Well according to Chipiko, as the song is
pretty old, Binks is someone who’s already

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:36.049
dead.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.200
Top candidates is whoever wrote the song.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.559
Joy Boy, the D Clan, the Kozuki or all of
them.

00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:48.329
Since sake has pretty strong symbolism in
Japan and is often associated with one’s

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:53.370
life’s story, Binks’ sake might actually
be delivering his story.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:59.560
This story, so the theory, is the true story
of the world, engraved in the Poneglyphs.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.470
There are 4 road Poneglyph leading to Laugh
tale, but more interesting are the 9 historical

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.029
ones, that contain the lost history.

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:12.999
According to this, delivering Bink’s sake
means nothing but finding all 9 historical

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:18.650
Poneglyphs, stringing them together and bringing
the full story with you to Laugh Tale.

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:24.970
And speaking of Robin, when Brook plays the
song, Robin mentions that she has heard it

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.699
somewhere before.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:29.840
On a pirate ship she traveled on maybe?

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:36.020
Only the word she uses in Japanese is natsukashii,
an adjective that can’t be translated literally

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.750
but expresses for memories of a time past.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:45.220
I think the only time Robin has genuinely
warm memories of is her time with the scholars.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.490
And if the heard the song in connection with
the research of Poneglyphs and the ancient

00:04:49.490 --> 00:05:00.389
Kingdoms, wouldn’t that make perfect sense?

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:03.650
The second interesting part of the first verse
is this.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:08.069
The birds sing as they draw circles in the
sky!

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:09.069
Circles?

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:10.069
Birds?

00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:11.069
Yup.

00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:16.729
This might very well be a reference to the
Kozuki themselves, the creators of the Poneglyphs.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:21.311
The bird in front of a circle that might very
well depict the sun as well as 4 arms, that

00:05:21.311 --> 00:05:23.740
remind us of the classical Jolly Roger.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:24.870
Keep that in mind.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.160
We’ll get back to this in a second.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.360
The last interesting line from the first verse
is this.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.909
Tsumugi no sato yo.

00:05:31.909 --> 00:05:35.340
Tsumugi no sato is the village of spinning.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.570
The village where what was spun?

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:42.699
Well, why not the story that is engraved in
the Poneglyphs.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:48.300
This would mean that the village talked about
in this old song would be no other place than

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.970
Wano itself, where we are right now.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.099
The place where the Poneglyphs were crafted
going all the way back to the void century.

00:05:55.099 --> 00:05:56.419
Are you warmed up already?

00:05:56.419 --> 00:06:05.020
Good, let’s go to verse 2, cause we’re
just getting started.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.729
After introducing the Binks, the Kozuki and
their story in the first verse, the second

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:13.699
one seems to describe some of that history
we just talked about.

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:17.650
We start with this: Warera kaizoku, umi watteku.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.219
This is translated as as ‘We are pirates
sailing through the Sea!’

00:06:21.219 --> 00:06:23.729
This is however too liberal.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:28.530
Watteku doesn’t mean sail, but to cut or
to divide.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:32.430
Thus the line actually says, we are pirates,
parting the sea.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:37.469
Now, yes, this could just mean cutting through
the waves with the ship, but what if this

00:06:37.469 --> 00:06:39.229
was way more literal?

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:41.370
Remember the Kozuki crest?

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:48.069
What if the pirates did indeed cut the sea,
let’s say in 4 pieces using a giant continent

00:06:48.069 --> 00:06:50.090
and a dangerous current.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.070
And could the X be the literal location of
laugh Tale?

00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:00.520
The backside of reverse mountain, that technically
IS the end of the grand line.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.949
The song continues: Now comes a storm through
the far-off sky.

00:07:04.949 --> 00:07:08.550
It seems that this seems to be a reference
to war.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.490
The storm brewing being a metaphor for conflict.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:17.050
Thus, this line might tell us about a war
in the past or in the future, related to the

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:22.870
island.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:33.660
The verse then ends with If you lose your
nerve this breath could be your last.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.319
But if you just hold on, The morning sun will
rise.

00:07:37.319 --> 00:07:40.499
This seems to refer to the dangers associated
with Laugh tale.

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:45.460
It tells us that people have failed before
and died trying.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.830
But even though people failed, th morning
sun will rise.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:52.580
The morning sun, in other words the dawn.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:58.470
Thus, the person who will bring the dawn will
one day appear after many others have failed.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:05.530
This my dear viewers is nothing else but the
theme of inherited will.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:10.760
And speaking of leading themes in the story,
the third verse starts talking about the dreams

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.520
of today and tomorrow.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.889
Once again this is inherited will and passing
on dreams to another.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:21.169
In this case it specifically means the dream
of those who originally wrote that song.

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:25.270
It continues: Te o furu kage nimo mou aenai
yo.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:30.610
Once again the English translation misses
out here The line literally translates not

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:35.669
to Waving our goodbyes, we’ll never meet
again, but to this: even though I will never

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:37.560
meet the waving shadow.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.650
This seems to refer to someone living in the
present.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.290
Only the living can cast shadows.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:48.690
And so it directly addresses the one who has
inherited their dream in that moment in time.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.480
But don't look so down, For tomorrow night
the moon will also rise!

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:53.620
Ah the moon.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:59.230
I actually made an entire video about its
meaning and connection to the past some time

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.350
ago.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:04.160
If you’re interested I’ll link that for
you up here.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:09.800
Apart from its heavy symbolism, what it actually
reminds me off is ‘Your Name’.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.960
In case you haven’t seen the movie.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.300
Watch it.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.210
It’s fantastic.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.960
But if you don’t want to get spoiled skip
20 seconds ahead.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.810
In the story, at dusk when the moon comes
up, it’s possible for two worlds to touch.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:30.250
So that might already be a hint that laugh
tale is only accessible during a certain time

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:33.430
period at dusk and again at dawn.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:39.020
The song ends with this: Doesn't matter who
you are, Someday you'll just be bones.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.160
Never-Ending, Ever-wandering, Our funny Traveling
tale!

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.920
What this line says is that everyone will
have to die some day and the song ends by

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.440
literally name-dropping laugh tale.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.640
The never ending, ever-wandering island.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.840
So what’s the big payoff here?

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:59.210
Hold thight.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:02.950
Laugh Tale is the land of the dead.

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:05.480
This is where people go in One Piece.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:06.770
How do we know?

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:07.770
Brook.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:11.680
Thanks to his fruit, he was able to return
from the land of the dead.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:14.460
He is the one that first plays the song.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:18.200
The first crew member Luffy wants is a musician.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:22.360
Brook introduces us to the concept of Souls
and soul power.

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:25.890
His clash with Big Mom is all about the soul.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:26.890
But wait.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.090
There’s more.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:36.690
Remember Rayleigh saying that they were to
early when they reached Laugh tale?

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:46.950
Well, the simple answer to this might be:
They weren’t dead yet.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:52.940
Roger also told his first mate that he would
never die and gladly handed himself in to

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:54.300
be executed.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.790
The D. Don’t fear death and greet it with
a smile.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.620
They are connected to the land of the dead.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.480
And is it really a coincidence that they were
singing the song on Roger’s ship when they

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.520
traveled to Laugh tale in chapter 967?

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.080
And I want you to take a look at this recent
panel here of Yasuie at his execution.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:17.070
The last thing he says is this: I will now
make my way to the other world.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.490
Let’s sing all together.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:22.640
Why would you sing when you are about to die?

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:26.040
Was Yasuie about to sing Bink’s sake?

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:36.160
In other words: Is it possible that Laugh
Tale is actually so difficult to reach, because

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.260
it means entering the realm of the dead?

00:11:38.260 --> 00:12:07.890
An island that only appears at dusk and that
you already have one chance 

00:12:07.890 --> 00:14:29.480
of enter

